<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Bernardi racing bicycle from around 1980 with Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport, high-flange hubs and Universal brakes."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/bernardi-ca-1980/hero.webp"/><title>Bernardi c. 1980 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/bernardi-ca-1980/"/><main class="atala-page"><header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">Previously in the collection · Italy · around 1980</p><h1>Bernardi</h1><p>A turquoise Italian steel racer with Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport, high-flange hubs and Universal brakes.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Turquoise Bernardi racing bicycle from around 1980"/><figcaption>The bicycle after servicing and restoration. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><section class="atala-facts"><div><strong>c. 1980</strong><span>Probable period</span></div><div><strong>53 cm</strong><span>Centre to top</span></div><div><strong>Campagnolo</strong><span>Nuovo Gran Sport</span></div><div><strong>Universal</strong><span>Rim brakes</span></div></section><section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Origins</p><h2>Possibly from Guido De Bernardi’s workshop</h2></div><div><p>The bicycle has been linked to Italian framebuilder Guido De Bernardi, but no catalogue, frame number or contemporary record securely confirms it. The attribution remains probable rather than established.</p><p>The visible evidence is clearer: a lugged Italian road frame with external cables, down-tube friction shifters and chromed parts.</p></div></section><section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/headbadge.webp`} alt="Bernardi head badge"/><figcaption>The badge in the original turquoise paint.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/derailleur.webp`} alt="Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport derailleur"/><figcaption>Nuovo Gran Sport rear derailleur and six-speed freewheel.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/front-brake.webp`} alt="Universal Super 68 front brake"/><figcaption>An Italian alternative to Campagnolo brakes.</figcaption></figure></section><section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">Nuovo Gran Sport</p><h2>Campagnolo without the top-group price</h2></div><div><p>The drivetrain used the familiar parallelogram derailleur, double chainset and friction controls. There were no clicks: the rider moved a hand to the down tube and found the correct chain position.</p><p>The restoration retained the original components and paint. New pads and nearly new tyres made the bicycle usable without making it look new.</p></div></section><section class="koga-specs"><div><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Lugged steel</dd></div><div><dt>Gears</dt><dd>Campagnolo Nuovo Gran Sport</dd></div><div><dt>Brakes</dt><dd>Universal Super 68</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Previously in the collection</dd></div></dl></section><section class="atala-closing"><p>The photographs preserve what can be said securely: a well-kept Italian racer from around 1980. Its exact origin remains open.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1980-1989/`}>View the period →</a></div></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
